{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"agent_message","text":"**REJECT for integration.** The family accounts for all 20 listed arities, but its credit rules and proposed treatment of `registered-definition?/1` do not match the current harness. Those differences could produce credit without the claimed exact-arity evidence.\n\n1. **Negative rows can credit arities under the current harness.** The document says they never do, including at [h3_3_fixture_family.md:88](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:88). But [kernel-exercise-entries](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1931) checks `:pass`, the call head, and traces; it does not exclude `:negative?` rows. A negative direct fixture that passes and records its target in both lanes can therefore receive direct credit. The preparation should state the actual rule and resolve whether integration must change the harness or accept that behavior.\n\n2. **The proposed `registered-definition?` mapping is not scoped to upstream `/1`.** The context function’s two-argument form represents an explicit-context lookup, while upstream `/1` supplies `empty-env` and upstream `/2` accepts an environment. Adding a global `+1` mapping as proposed at [h3_3_fixture_family.md:687](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:687) makes the trace matcher apply that offset to *every* upstream arity. Consequently, a bare `[registered-definition? 2]` trace from another named integration can be attributed to upstream `/1` even when it arose from an explicit-environment `/2` call; `[registered-definition? 3]` has a similar ambiguity for `/2`. The existing unregister precedent does not establish that this multi-arity mapping preserves identity. Any harness change needs a separately reviewed, arity- and call-origin-safe design.\n\n   The document also says F2 needs named-integration registration for conditional credit. Once the proposed mapping exists, however, F2’s upstream `:call` head and argument count meet the harness’s **direct** criterion; its `:guest-call` trace can satisfy the mapped trace check without named registration. See [h3_3_fixture_family.md:645](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:645) and [kernel-exercise-entries](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle_test.clj:1931).\n\nThe rest of the static review supports the preparation with these limits:\n\n- The [credit table](/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-h32/test/yang/latte_oracle/h3_3_fixture_family.md:651) accounts for every entry in the checked-in unexercised inventory and specifies a required record in both C lanes. Those records and B/C outcomes remain predictions, not observed evidence.\n- The pinned sources confirm that `mk-fresh` is deterministic and that `readable-term-` is private. They also confirm that upstream `registered-definition?/1` uses `empty-env`, with namespace resolution as a fallback. Keeping F2 non-crediting and the gate blocked is therefore the honest default pending an approved treatment.\n- The verifier’s 43 checks correspond to the documented A cases, including the additional unparser registry-shape check. Its dependency pin matches `86fca0d1`. I did not independently execute it or verify the reported 43/43 run.\n- The commit diff from `24ed231a` contains only the two stated new files; corpus, declarations, evidence, and harness are untouched. The worktree also has two untracked brief files, which are outside that commit.\n\nNo suites or verifier were run in this review."}}
